Subject: [PATCH] net: macb: restart the transmit queues after a link flap

The GEM driver can stall a transmit queue for good when the link goes
down and up while frames are still in its ring.  macb_mac_link_down()
masks the TX interrupts and clears TE.  On link-up,
gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring() rotates each ring so that the oldest frame
not yet reaped sits at index 0, and macb_mac_link_up() sets TE again.
Measured on the Zynq UltraScale+ GEM (two queues) and the Zynq-7000 GEM
(one), each under saturating load across a restart of autonegotiation,
three states stall a queue, and each needs its own part of this patch.

1. TBQP, the controller's pointer into the ring, is not set back to the
   ring base.  Both TRMs say clearing TE resets it (UG1085 v2.5, ch. 34,
   TX Buffers; UG585 v1.7, 16.2.5, Tx Buffers, and the tx_en bit), but
   its read-back stayed in place after TE was cleared in 13 of 20
   link-downs on one UltraScale+ queue (none of 20 on the other) and in
   5 of 25 on the Zynq-7000, and the controller then sat past the
   pending frames (one queue's pointer at index 397 with its frames at
   387 to 396).  TBQP is now written to the ring base on every path of
   the shuffle.  This is the upstream patch "net: macb: reprogram TBQP
   after shuffling the TX ring on link-up" (netdev, July 2026,
   https://lkml.iu.edu/2607.0/11020.html; Fixes: 881a0263d502 "net:
   macb: Shuffle the tx ring before enabling tx"), here also on the path
   where no rotation is needed, which upstream leaves alone.

2. TSTART is not written, so with the pointer at the frames the
   transmitter stays idle, and the stack, holding the queue stopped on
   those frames' bytes, sends nothing that would write it.  The last
   hunk writes TSTART once after TE is set if any queue still holds
   frames.  It is not from upstream.

3. A frame the controller sent before TE was cleared has TX_USED set in
   its first descriptor (the TRMs: written back to the first buffer of a
   frame once it is transmitted) but is not reaped, the TX interrupts
   being masked first.  Such frames led a ring at link-up when the
   link-down was handled late: with 20 ms between the masking and the
   clearing of TE, at 19 of 20 link-ups on one UltraScale+ ring and 9 of
   20 on the Zynq-7000; with 30 us, at 11 of 30 on the Zynq-7000, each
   time followed by frames not yet sent; and with no delay added, on the
   Zynq-7000 at the first flap without this patch and at the third with
   parts 1 and 2 alone.  Rotated to the base, the first of them is the
   descriptor the restarted controller reads: it stops there at once
   (TXUBR; the TRMs: transmission halts when a descriptor with its used
   bit set is read), the driver then reaps those frames, and its tail
   passes the controller's pointer.  Every later TSTART, which
   macb_tx_restart() writes on each TXUBR, reads the same descriptor: no
   frame is sent again, and TXUBR interrupts come at 100,000 to 250,000
   a second.  Under parts 1 and 2 alone this followed each of the three
   logged link-ups that found such frames, and none of the 71 that did
   not.  The shuffle now rotates the first frame without TX_USED to the
   base and leaves the sent frames just behind it, at the end of the
   ring, where macb_tx_complete() reaps them.  They raise no TCOMP, and
   only queue 0 has TXUBR (UG1087, int_q1_status; UG1085, Priority
   Queuing: TXUBR is a resource error, raised once every queue's next
   descriptor is TX_USED), so a second queue whose frames had all been
   sent stayed stopped on its byte limit, never reaped: measured at the
   second flap with 2 ms between the masking and the clearing of TE, and
   at the first or second with the TX polls held off across the
   link-down.  The TSTART therefore comes with a run of every queue's TX
   poll; with the polls so held off, 20 of 20 flaps then drained, such a
   queue found at 19 of the link-ups.

Measured with the flap test (autonegotiation restarted under saturating
UDP load; each queue's in-flight bytes back to 0 within 6 s): without
this patch both boards stalled at the first flap.  With parts 1 and 2
alone (this patch's previous form) the UltraScale+ drained 100 of 100
flaps, but with the link-down handled 20 ms late both stalled in state
3 (the UltraScale+ at the first flap, the Zynq-7000 at the fifth).
With this patch every flap drained: 100 of 100 on each board, and 20
of 20 on each with the link-down handled 20 ms late.  Without a delay,
the frames still in a ring when TE was cleared were all unsent at
link-up (20 link-downs on the UltraScale+, 25 on the Zynq-7000), and
they were sent after the restart.

Not fixed here: macb_mac_link_down() clears TE without bp->lock, and a
TSTART that macb_start_xmit() writes at the same time can set it again
(measured twice in about 250 link-downs; the queues drained).

Applied to Buildroot's mainline kernel by BR2_GLOBAL_PATCH_DIR.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
@@ -675,7 +675,7 @@ static void gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring(stru
 	unsigned int head, tail, count, ring_size, desc_size;
 	struct macb_tx_skb tx_skb, *skb_curr, *skb_next;
 	struct macb_dma_desc *desc_curr, *desc_next;
-	unsigned int i, cycles, shift, curr, next;
+	unsigned int i, cycles, shift, curr, next, start;
 	struct macb *bp = queue->bp;
 	unsigned char desc[24];
 	unsigned long flags;
@@ -691,16 +691,33 @@ static void gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring(stru
 	ring_size = bp->tx_ring_size;
 	count = CIRC_CNT(head, tail, ring_size);
 
-	if (!(tail % ring_size))
-		goto unlock;
+	/* A frame the controller sent before TE was cleared has TX_USED set
+	 * in its first descriptor but may not have been reaped, as the TX
+	 * interrupts were masked at link-down. The controller starts again
+	 * at the ring base and stops at the first TX_USED descriptor it reads
+	 * there, so rotate the first frame not yet sent to the base and leave
+	 * the sent ones just behind it, at the end of the ring, for
+	 * macb_tx_complete() to reap.
+	 */
+	rmb();
+	for (start = tail; start != head; start++) {
+		if (!(macb_tx_desc(queue, start)->ctrl & MACB_BIT(TX_USED)))
+			break;
+		/* skb is set for the last buffer of the frame */
+		while (!macb_tx_skb(queue, start)->skb && start + 1 != head)
+			start++;
+	}
+
+	if (!(start % ring_size))
+		goto reset_hw_ptr;
 
 	if (!count) {
 		queue->tx_head = 0;
 		queue->tx_tail = 0;
-		goto unlock;
+		goto reset_hw_ptr;
 	}
 
-	shift = tail % ring_size;
+	shift = start % ring_size;
 	cycles = gcd(ring_size, shift);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < cycles; i++) {
@@ -740,13 +757,19 @@ static void gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring(stru
 		       sizeof(struct macb_tx_skb));
 	}
 
-	queue->tx_head = count;
-	queue->tx_tail = 0;
+	queue->tx_head = head - start;
+	queue->tx_tail = tail - start;
 
 	/* Make descriptor updates visible to hardware */
 	wmb();
 
-unlock:
+reset_hw_ptr:
+	/* The ring base now holds the first frame not yet sent, or the end
+	 * of the queue, so TBQP must be reprogrammed to match; otherwise it
+	 * keeps pointing at a stale descriptor. Safe to write directly here
+	 * as TX is still disabled (called from macb_mac_link_up() before TE).
+	 */
+	queue_writel(queue, TBQP, lower_32_bits(queue->tx_ring_dma));
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
 }
 
@@ -823,6 +846,34 @@ static void macb_mac_link_up(struct phyl
 
 	macb_writel(bp, NCR, ctrl | MACB_BIT(RE) | MACB_BIT(TE));
 
+	/* Frames queued before the link went down are still in the rings,
+	 * now at their bases, and the transmitter was enabled idle: start it,
+	 * since no new frame may come to do so while the stack holds the
+	 * queues stopped on those frames' bytes. Frames it sent before TE
+	 * was cleared raise no TCOMP, and only queue 0 can raise TXUBR:
+	 * poll every queue so that those frames are reaped.
+	 */
+	if (!(bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_MACB_IS_EMAC)) {
+		bool pending = false;
+
+		for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue) {
+			spin_lock_irqsave(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+			if (queue->tx_head != queue->tx_tail)
+				pending = true;
+			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+		}
+		if (pending) {
+			spin_lock_irqsave(&bp->lock, flags);
+			macb_writel(bp, NCR, macb_readl(bp, NCR) | MACB_BIT(TSTART));
+			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&bp->lock, flags);
+
+			local_bh_disable();
+			for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue)
+				napi_schedule(&queue->napi_tx);
+			local_bh_enable();
+		}
+	}
+
 	netif_tx_wake_all_queues(ndev);
 }
 
